  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDeep layer moisture and humidity increases Thu into Fri, as a \nsouthwest flow continues around the periphery of a Bermuda high \nin the western Atlantic. Dewpoints will likely rise into the \nlower 70s to mid 70s(southern areas), with PWAT anomalies \nincreasing to +1 to +3 STDEV(from NAEFS). Convection will be \nmost likely during the diurnally favored afternoon through early\nevening hours. Synoptic scale forcing still looks fairly weak \nmuch of the time, as a frontal system will be positioned \nnorth/west of Lake Ontario. The front will only make \nslow/gradual southeast progress due to the Bermuda high in \nplace. Will continue to cap PoPs at 60-70% Thu and Fri. Both \ndays should not be washouts, although scattered to numerous \nshowers/T- storm are expected to develop in the afternoon. \n\nSBCAPE forecast from the REFS looks more robust now, with \npotentially 2000-3000 J/Kg from around the Capital District \nsouth along with 0-6 km shear of 25-30 kt. This environment is \nconducive for some severe storms, although lack of significant \nforcing is a mitigating factor along with fairly weak mid level\nlapse rates. The main threat would be isolated damaging wind \ngusts due to precip loading. The Storm Prediction Center \ncontinues to have a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) outlook from \naround the Mohawk Valley and Saratoga region south and west.\n\nAI NWP convective hazards forecast continues to show potential \nfor some severe storms Fri, especially from Albany south/east. \nBased on the latest CAMs, timing looks to be later in the day \ninto the evening. CAPE looks similar to Thu, although shear may \nbe a bit weaker. With high PWATs continuing, there is potential \nfor isolated wet microbursts. Will continue to monitor trends.\n\nAlso with a humid/moist environment and wet antecedent ground \nconditions in place, there is the potential for isolated flash \nflooding where persistent or repeated downpours occur. There is \na Marginal Risk (level 1 of 4) of excessive rainfall both Thu \nand Fri.\n\nThe front may finally start to settle south/east across the \narea on Sat, with additional showers/T-storms again mainly in \nthe afternoon to early evening hours. Again, storms may produce \ngusty winds and locally heavy rainfall. A small area of high \npressure builds in on Sun, providing drier conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith our region positioned along the northwest periphery of the\nBermuda high, increasing heat and humidity will occur Thu into \nFri. Although high temperatures will likely remain below 90F, \ndewpoints rising into the lower/mid 70s will still result in \nheat indices(feels-like temperatures) in the mid to upper 90s in\nsouthern portions of the mid Hudson Valley and southern \nLitchfield CT both days. A Heat Advisory has been issued for \nthese few zones late Thu morning through early Fri evening. In \nvalley locations outside of the advisory area, peak heat indices\nwill mainly in the lower 90s.",
